Letting $x=-4$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-4+3)^n}{n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-1)^n}{n} \end{align}$

This is the alternating harmonic series, which is known to converge.

Letting $x=-2$, we get the series:

$\begin{align}
\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{(-2+3)^n}{n}
&=\displaystyle\sum\limits_{n=1}^{\infty } \dfrac{1}{n} \end{align}$

This is the harmonic series, which is known to diverge.
